At 38k rows on Postgres 16, this doesn't need a separate search service — Postgres full-text search on the existing `products` table is enough.

Add a generated `tsvector` column weighting `name` above `description`, GIN-indexed, and query it with `websearch_to_tsquery` ordered by `ts_rank`:

```sql
ALTER TABLE products ADD COLUMN search_vector tsvector
  GENERATED ALWAYS AS (
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', name), 'A') ||
    setweight(to_tsvector('english', description), 'B')
  ) STORED;

CREATE INDEX products_search_idx ON products USING GIN (search_vector);
```

Then a new route mirroring the existing paged list in `routes/products.js:7`:

```js
router.get("/search", async (req, res) => {
  const q = (req.query.q || "").trim();
  if (!q) return res.json([]);
  const rows = await db.query(
    `SELECT id, sku, name, price_cents,
            ts_rank(search_vector, websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)) AS rank
     FROM products
     WHERE active AND search_vector @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english', $1)
     ORDER BY rank DESC LIMIT 50`,
    [q]
  );
  res.json(rows);
});
```

The one real fork: typo tolerance. Postgres FTS matches stemmed words, not misspellings — "sofa" won't match "sofah". If fuzzy matching turns out to matter, that's `pg_trgm` with a similarity threshold, not a new service. Leave that out until you see it's needed.

Key by `client_id`, not IP — your own comment in `routes/api.js:7-8` says several clients can share a NAT address, so IP-based limiting would throttle innocent bystanders instead of the offender. `req.client.client_id` is already resolved by the auth middleware before it hits any route.

Store the counters in Redis, not process memory — `app.js` runs 4 clustered workers per box with nothing shared between them except Redis (per the comment in `redis.js:3-4`), so an in-memory counter would let each worker/box give the client its own separate quota. Redis is already wired up for sessions, so reuse `redis.js`'s `client`.

Use an atomic fixed-window counter (INCR + PEXPIRE NX in one round trip) rather than a naive GET-then-SET, to avoid a race where concurrent requests from the same client both read a stale count and both pass. Sketch, added right after `req.client` is set in `routes/api.js:17`:

```js
const key = `rl:${req.client.client_id}`;
const count = await redisClient.incr(key);
if (count === 1) await redisClient.pexpire(key, 60_000);
if (count > LIMIT_PER_PLAN[req.client.plan]) {
  res.set("Retry-After", "60");
  return res.status(429).end();
}
```

Keying the limit off `req.client.plan` (already returned by the api_keys query) lets different tiers get different ceilings for free. A sliding-window log would smooth out edge-of-window bursts but costs a sorted-set per client instead of one counter — not worth it unless the fixed window proves gameable in practice.

