Key fork: what to key the limiter on. `routes/api.js` already resolves `req.client.client_id` from the API key before anything else runs, and the comment there says clients often share a NAT IP — so IP-based limiting would throttle innocent clients sharing that address while doing nothing to stop a client that rotates IPs. Key on `client_id`.

Second fork: where the counter lives. `app.js` forks 4 workers per box with nothing shared in module scope, so an in-process counter (a `Map`, a token-bucket object) only limits each worker independently — the offending client can still burst 4x per box before any single worker notices. `redis.js` already gives every worker a shared client for sessions; reuse it so the count is global across workers and boxes.

Mechanism: a fixed-window counter per client, incremented atomically and compared against a limit derived from `req.client.plan` (the `plan` column is already in the `api_keys` row you fetch). `INCR` plus a separate `EXPIRE` is racy if the process dies between the two calls, so do it in one Lua script (or `redis.call('INCR', ...)` + `redis.call('PEXPIRE', ..., 'NX')` in the same script) so a window always self-expires. On overflow, respond `429` with `Retry-After`.

```js
// routes/api.js, added right after the API-key middleware
const { client: redis } = require("../redis");

const LIMITS = { free: 60, pro: 600 }; // requests per minute

const RATE_LIMIT_SCRIPT = `
  local count = redis.call('INCR', KEYS[1])
  if count == 1 then redis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], ARGV[1]) end
  return count
`;

router.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const windowMs = 60_000;
  const key = `rl:${req.client.client_id}:${Math.floor(Date.now() / windowMs)}`;
  const count = await redis.eval(RATE_LIMIT_SCRIPT, 1, key, windowMs);
  const limit = LIMITS[req.client.plan] ?? LIMITS.free;
  if (count > limit) {
    res.set("Retry-After", "60");
    return res.status(429).end();
  }
  next();
});
```

Fixed window over sliding log/token bucket: it under-counts at window boundaries (a client can do ~2x limit across a boundary), but it's one Redis round-trip and no extra state to reason about — good enough for "stop one client from taking the service down," not for billing-grade precision.

Read the actual setup: `app.js` forks 4 worker processes per box with no shared memory, `redis.js` already gives every worker a shared store (used for sessions), and `routes/api.js` resolves `X-Api-Key` to a `client_id` and `plan` in middleware before hitting Postgres.

Two decisions actually fork this design:

**Key by `client_id`, not IP.** The auth middleware comment already says one client uses many addresses and several clients can share one address behind a corporate NAT — an IP-keyed limiter would throttle innocent clients sharing that NAT while letting the abusive client route around it from a second address. `req.client.client_id` is already resolved by the time you'd want to check the limit.

**Count in Redis, not in-process.** With 4 cluster workers and nothing shared in module scope, an in-memory counter gives each worker its own quota — a client hammering the LB gets ~4x the intended limit, split across workers essentially at random. Redis is already the one thing all workers share, so use it rather than adding new infrastructure.

Given that, the shape: a fixed-window or sliding-window counter, incremented atomically per request, keyed `rl:{client_id}:{window}`, limit chosen from `req.client.plan`. Enforced as a second middleware in `routes/api.js`, right after the existing auth middleware and before either route handler — so a hammering client gets rejected before the request reaches Postgres at all, which is the actual "degrading it for everybody else" mechanism (shared DB pool/connections, not just CPU).

Sketch of the enforcement point:

```js
router.use(async (req, res, next) => {
  const key = `rl:${req.client.client_id}`;
  const limit = PLAN_LIMITS[req.client.plan]; // requests per window
  const count = await redis.client.incr(key);
  if (count === 1) await redis.client.expire(key, WINDOW_SECONDS);
  if (count > limit) {
    res.set("Retry-After", String(await redis.client.ttl(key)));
    return res.status(429).end();
  }
  next();
});
```

`INCR`+conditional `EXPIRE` is a known race (two requests can both hit `count === 1`) — fine at this scale, but if it matters, replace with a small Lua script (`EVAL`) so the increment-and-maybe-set-TTL is atomic. That atomicity-vs-simplicity tradeoff, and window algorithm choice (fixed vs. sliding vs. token bucket), are the next level of detail down once you confirm this shape.

